6. MULTIPLE OPENAI-COMPATIBLE API CONFIGURATIONS

The current AI Studio displays an OpenAI / Compatible API option.

Keep that integration, but improve it to support multiple independently configured providers.

A single OpenAI-compatible integration must not mean a single saved API key or a single base URL.

Users must be able to configure multiple compatible services simultaneously.

Examples include compatible services that use different:

- Base URLs
- API keys
- Model names
- Authentication conventions
- Rate limits
- Context limits
- Request formats
- Usage pricing

Do not hardcode these examples as if they were guaranteed to have identical APIs.

6.1 AI Provider Center

Create a dedicated provider-management screen.

Include:

- Add Provider
- Edit Provider
- Duplicate Configuration
- Delete Provider
- Enable or Disable
- Set as Default
- Test Connection
- Refresh Models where supported
- Select Model
- Configure Capabilities
- View Connection Status

The user must be able to save multiple configurations of the OpenAI-compatible provider type.

For example, a user might configure:

- Compatible Provider A with its own base URL, key, and model
- Compatible Provider B with a different base URL, key, and model
- Compatible Provider C with another endpoint and configuration

All three must remain saved independently.

The exact provider names are user-defined.

6.2 Provider Configuration Fields

For compatible APIs, support the applicable fields:

- Display Name
- Provider Type
- Base URL
- API Key
- Model ID
- Optional Organization or Project ID when required
- Optional supported custom headers
- Request Timeout
- Maximum Retry Count
- Context Limit when known
- Default Sampling Parameters when supported
- Streaming Support
- Tool Calling Support
- Structured Output Support
- Provider Notes
- Enabled Status

Do not require every provider to support every field.

Display advanced options only when useful.

Validate URLs and required fields.

Do not silently append an endpoint path that creates an invalid URL.

Normalize base URLs carefully and document the expected format.

6.3 Model Discovery

Provide a Refresh Models button when the provider supports model discovery.

If model discovery is unsupported or fails, allow the user to enter a model ID manually.

Do not claim that a model is available merely because its name was entered.

Distinguish:

- Connection successful
- Authentication failed
- Model list retrieved
- Model list unavailable
- Model not verified
- Provider unavailable

6.4 Per-Task Provider Selection

Allow independent default configurations for:

- Text Generation
- Script Writing
- Subtitle Assistance
- Image Generation
- Video Generation
- Audio Generation
- Speech-to-Text
- Text-to-Speech

A provider must only be offered for a task when it advertises or has been configured for the necessary capability.

For example, a text-only endpoint must not be shown as a working video-generation provider.

6.5 Request Routing

Create a provider registry that resolves requests to the selected configuration.

Each configuration must have its own:

- Stable ID
- Display name
- Base URL
- Secure credential reference
- Model configuration
- Capability declaration
- Request settings
- Connection status

The selected configuration must determine which credentials, endpoint, and model are used.

Never accidentally use Provider A's API key when sending a request through Provider B.

Avoid global singleton variables containing one mutable API key or base URL for all providers.

6.6 Secure Credential Storage

Use secure, Android-appropriate credential storage.

Requirements:

- Store keys encrypted.
- Use Android Keystore-backed key management where appropriate.
- Never store API keys in ordinary project metadata.
- Never expose API keys in logs.
- Never display the full key after saving.
- Allow replacing or deleting saved keys.
- Ensure provider duplication does not unintentionally copy secrets without an explicit user decision.
- Ensure exports and backups do not leak credentials.
- Avoid logging sensitive request headers.

6.7 Provider Testing

The Test Connection action should make an actual request supported by that provider.

Report real results.

Examples:

- Connection successful
- Invalid API key
- Invalid base URL
- Model unavailable
- Unsupported endpoint
- Rate limit exceeded
- Request timed out
- Provider returned an error

Never display a fake success message.

6.8 AI Usage and Cost

Show available usage information when the provider exposes it.

Where pricing is known, provide a reasonable estimate before expensive operations.

Where pricing is unknown, display that the cost cannot be verified.

Allow users to choose a default provider per task.

Support manual switching between providers.

Do not require the user to re-enter an API key every time they change models within a saved configuration.

